# zed.rc.local — the NAS-specific settings in /etc/zfs/zed.d/zed.rc.
#
# THIS FILE IS A RECORD, NOT SOMETHING THAT GETS INSTALLED. zed reads exactly
# one config, /etc/zfs/zed.d/zed.rc, and there is no .d/ include mechanism — so
# these three lines have to be edited into that file by hand. This copy exists
# so that when an OpenZFS upgrade overwrites zed.rc, you know what was in it.
#
# ############################################################################
# # zed.rc IS A PACKAGE FILE. An OpenZFS upgrade replaces it, which silently  #
# # switches notifications off. Nothing warns you. Re-apply after every       #
# # upgrade — it is in the standing checks in the nas note for that reason.   #
# ############################################################################
#
# Apply with (line numbers as of zfs-2.4.1-1, verify before trusting them):
#   sudo cp /etc/zfs/zed.d/zed.rc /etc/zfs/zed.d/zed.rc.bak
#   sudo sed -i '' \
#     -e 's|^ZED_SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL="\(.*\)"$|ZED_SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL="\1/slack"|' \
#     -e 's|^#ZED_NOTIFY_VERBOSE=0$|ZED_NOTIFY_VERBOSE=1|' \
#     -e 's|^ZED_EMAIL_ADDR="root"$|#ZED_EMAIL_ADDR="root"|' \
#     /etc/zfs/zed.d/zed.rc
#   sudo chmod 600 /etc/zfs/zed.d/zed.rc
# The first expression is idempotent ONLY if the URL does not already end in
# /slack — check with grep before re-running it.

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Discord, through the Slack backend.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# zed has no Discord backend, but Discord webhooks expose a Slack-compatible
# endpoint: append /slack and it accepts Slack's payload, which is what
# zed_notify_slack_webhook() already sends:
#     {"text": "*<subject>*\n```<body>```"}
#
# THE /slack SUFFIX IS NOT OPTIONAL, and getting it wrong fails silently.
# Measured 2026-08-23 against this exact webhook:
#     .../<id>/<token>/slack   -> HTTP 200, body "ok"           delivered
#     .../<id>/<token>         -> HTTP 400, "Cannot send an empty message"
# The bare endpoint is Discord's native API, which expects "content" and not
# "text", so it reads zed's payload as empty. And zed does NOT notice: it greps
# the response for Slack's {"error": {"message": ...}} shape, Discord returns a
# flat {"message": ...}, curl exits 0 because there is no -f — so a rejected
# post is counted as a success. The URL was configured without the suffix at
# first and every notification went nowhere while looking fine.
#
# The URL is a bearer credential: anyone holding it can post to the server.
# Hence chmod 600, and keep a copy in a password manager.
ZED_SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL="https://discord.com/api/webhooks/<id>/<token>/slack"

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Verbose, deliberately against the default of 0.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# At 0, scrub_finish-notify.sh exits early when `zpool status -x` says the pool
# is healthy, so you hear only about problems. That is the right default in
# general and the wrong one here, because of the failure mode above: this
# channel can die without telling anyone. If silence means both "healthy" and
# "the webhook is broken", it means nothing.
#
# At 1 the monthly scrub always posts, so the message is a heartbeat and its
# ABSENCE on the 1st is the signal. Cost is roughly twelve messages a year.
ZED_NOTIFY_VERBOSE=1

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Email off.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shipped as ZED_EMAIL_ADDR="root". /usr/bin/mail exists on macOS, so zed
# happily delivers to a local mailbox nobody has ever opened — a notification
# that is worse than none, because it counts as a success in zed's tally.
# Commented out entirely, which makes zed_notify_email() return 2 = skipped
# rather than a bogus delivery.
#ZED_EMAIL_ADDR="root"

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Left at defaults, noted so the choice is visible
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ZED_NOTIFY_INTERVAL_SECS=3600 — rate limit per event class. Fine: a monthly
#   scrub cannot trip it, and for a genuinely flapping device an hour is the
#   right amount of quiet.
# ZED_USE_ENCLOSURE_LEDS=1 — already on, and already known to be useless on this
#   enclosure. The 2026-08-17 pull test showed the Orico LEDs signal presence,
#   not fault. Harmless, so left alone.
# ZED_SCRUB_AFTER_RESILVER — leave off. resilver_finish-start-scrub.sh is
#   enabled in zed.d, and on this hardware a resilver already implies something
#   odd happened at the bridge; queueing a 20-hour scrub straight afterwards is
#   a decision to make by hand, not automatically.
